1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ## Overview: Timurid (Mughal) Lineage and Y-DNA Claims
2 CONT
2 CONT In online genealogy and genetics discussions, the Timurid (Mughal) dynasty is **sometimes** associated with Y-DNA haplogroup **C2**, but it is important to understand the **nature and limits of this claim**.
2 CONT
2 CONT ### What is often claimed
2 CONT
2 CONT * Timur (Tamerlane) and his descendants are frequently *assumed* to belong to Y-DNA haplogroup **C2**
2 CONT * This assumption comes from:
2 CONT
2 CONT   * The association of Mongol expansions with C2
2 CONT   * The “Genghis Khan star cluster” hypothesis
2 CONT   * Modern DNA tests of some individuals who *claim* Timurid descent
2 CONT
2 CONT ### What is actually proven
2 CONT
2 CONT * **No ancient DNA has been published** from Timur, Babur, Akbar, or confirmed male-line Timurid remains
2 CONT * There is **no authoritative genetic study** that definitively assigns the Timurid male line to haplogroup C2
2 CONT * All C2 associations are **inferential**, not direct evidence
2 CONT
2 CONT ### Why the assumption exists
2 CONT
2 CONT * Timur belonged to the Barlas tribe, which had Mongol origins
2 CONT * Many Mongolic populations today show high frequencies of C2
2 CONT * Over time, this cultural and historical association has been simplified into a genetic assumption
2 CONT
2 CONT ### Why this matters for genealogy
2 CONT
2 CONT * Mughal-era “descent” does not always mean direct biological father-to-son lineage
2 CONT * Many families were connected to Mughal princes through:
2 CONT
2 CONT   * Maternal descent
2 CONT   * Household or foster relationships
2 CONT   * Military or administrative service
2 CONT * These connections were commonly recorded or remembered as “descent” in later family trees
2 CONT
2 CONT ### Summary
2 CONT
2 CONT > Claims that the Timurid or Mughal male line was Y-DNA C2 are **based on population inference and modern self-reported testing**, not on confirmed DNA from the dynasty itself.
2 CONT
2 CONT This distinction helps reconcile traditional Mughal genealogies with modern genetic evidence **without dismissing historical family connections**.
