1 NAME Pak-Ni'had Banu Begum
2 GIVN Pak-Ni'had Banu Begum
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1641
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Begum, the daughter of Dara Shikoh, was known as Zainab or Zainab al-Mulk. She married Shums Din (also referred to as Shams al-Din) in the 18th century, linking the Mughal dynasty with the Ratyal Mughal family. Thi
2 CONC s marriage symbolized the political and social alliances typical of the time, as prominent families often intermarried to strengthen their influence and status.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dara Shikoh, the eldest son of Shah Jahan, was known for his intellectual pursuits and for promoting a syncretic philosophy blending Hinduism and Islam. His daughter’s marriage to a member of the Ratyal Mughal community helped sol
2 CONC idify ties between the royal family and local noble families, which was a common practice among the Mughal elite to maintain power and extend their lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ratyal Mughals, who were of Mughal descent, established themselves in various regions and sought to connect with the Mughal nobility through such marriages, enhancing their own social standing. This kind of alliance would have
2 CONC  been beneficial for both families, reinforcing the prestige of the Ratyal Mughal lineage while offering the Mughal family continued influence in local governance and society. 
2 CONT
2 CONT If you are looking for more specific details regarding the lineage or additional historical context, feel free to ask!
