1 TITL Sayyid
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sayyid Ahmad Ali Khan (died 30 October 1824), popularly known as Walla Jah or Ahmad Ali Khan of Murshidabad, was the Nawab of Bengal and Bihar. He succeeded his half-brother, Zain-ud-Din Ali Khan, after he died on 
2 CONC 6 August 1821, without a male issue. Walla Jah was the Nawab of Bengal from 1821 to 1824..
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years
2 CONT Ahmad Ali Khan, better known as Walla Jah, was the second son of Baber Ali Khan by his second principal wife. He succeeded his half brother, Zain-ud-Din Ali Khan after his death on 6 August 1821 as the Nawab Nazim of Bengal and Bi
2 CONC har under the titles of Baeran ul-Mulk (Administrator of the country), Ihtisham ud-Daulah (Dignifier of the country), Walla Jah (Of High Rank) and Nahabat Jang (Horror in War).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and succession
2 CONT Walla Jah had a short reign of just three years from 1821 until his death on 30 October 1824 at Murshidabad Palace. He was buried at Jafarganj Cemetery and was succeeded by his only child, Mubarak Ali Khan II as the Nawab of Benga
2 CONC l and Bihar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT Principal wives
2 CONT Nawab Nazim Ahmad Ali Khan had only one principal wife as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT Nawab Najib-un-nisa Begum Sahiba. She was a Gaddinashin Begum. She died at Murshidabad Palace on 23 August 1858 and was buried at Jafarganj Cemetery.
2 CONT Mut‘ah wives
2 CONT The following is a list of the mut‘ah wives of Nawab Nazim Ahmad Ali Khan and some additional information about them:
2 CONT
2 CONT Name  : Misri Khanum (d. Before-23 September 1837)
2 CONT Name  : Fatima Khanum
2 CONT Name  : Bibi Rahim-un-nisa
2 CONT Name  : Bibi hayat-un-nisa
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT Walla Jah had only one child, who was born as a son by his only principal wife, Najib-un-nisa Begum. He was Mubarak Ali Khan II who succeeded Walla Jah as the Nawab Nazim of Bengal and Bihar.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahmad_Ali_Khan_of_Murshidabad
