1 TITL Sayyid
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ruler of Bengal
2 CONT Sayyid Mubaraq 'Ali Khan (s/o Luft un-nisa Begum). He d. after 1839, having had issue, one son and three daughters:
2 CONT (i) Sayyid Mahdi 'Ali Khan. He had issue, one son and one daughter:
2 CONT 1. Sayyid Saulat 'Ali Khan.
2 CONT 1. Mumtaz un-nisa Begum.
2 CONT (i) Badr un-nisa Begum.
2 CONT (ii) Husaini Begum. Copyright© Christopher Buyers
2 CONT (ii) Mubaraq un-nisa Begum.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.royalark.net/India4/murshid5.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mubarak_Ali_Khan_II
2 CONT Sayyid Mubarak Ali Khan II, popularly known as Humayun Jah (1810 – 1838), was born on 29 September 1810 to Ahmad Ali Khan and Nazib un-Nisa Begum. He was the Nawab of Bengal from 1824 to 1838. He was succeeded by Mansur Ali Khan. 
2 CONC He built the famous and renowned Hazarduari Palace and Mubarak Manzil in Murshidabad, India. Nawab Nazim Humayun Jah died on 3 October 1838.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Mubarak Ali Khan was the only son of Ahmad Ali Khan. He succeeded his father after he died in 1824 under the titles of Humayun Jah (Of Auspicious Rank), Shuja ul-Mulk (Hero of the Country), Ihtisham ud-Daulah (Victor in War) and F
2 CONC eroz-e-Jang (Dignifier of the Country).
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign as a Nawab
2 CONT In 1826 he went to Patna for a change in the dewanship and when he attained his majority he took the management in his own hands, with the plan to dismiss Raja Ganga Dhar from the Nizamat Dewanship.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Findall Bagh (now known as Mubarak Manzil) the Courts of Justice of the East India Company was erected, however, it was unused after the administration of law and justice was removed to Calcutta. The Sadar Dewani Adalat (courts
2 CONC  or darbars) were held here from 1765 to 1781 until it was removed to Calcutta. Subsequently, the Civil and Criminal Courts (which were located here) was transferred by Lord Cornwallis in 1793 by transferring the Supreme court to 
2 CONC Calcutta. This spacious property of Findall Bagh was abandoned. There were also three buildings on this spacious property.
2 CONT
2 CONT In May 1830 Nawab Humayun Jah bought this property from Raja Kissen Chand Bahadur and Kumar Chand of Nashipur for an amount of ₹ 35,000. He transformed the property into a pleasure garden and erected the Moti Mahal (also known as 
2 CONC the Red Bunglow) here, he named the garden Mubarak Manzil.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the terrace in front of Moti Mahal stood the Royal Throne (black throne) used by the Nawab "Nazims" of Bengal from the time of Shah Shuja. It was brought here by Nawab Nazim Humayun Jah. This Royal Throne is round in shape and 
2 CONC is made up of black stone. The throne is 6 feet (72 inches) in diameter and 1.5 feet (18 inches) high. It was made at Mongyer, Bihar by Khwaja Nazar of Bokhara in 1643 AD. Robert Clive placed Mir Jafar on this throne at Mansurganj
2 CONC  after the Battle of Plassey in 1757 AD. Clive to sat on it side-by-side with Najimuddin Ali Khan at Motijhil when celebrating the pooneath after the acquisition of the Dewani by the East India Company. The throne is now kept in V
2 CONC ictoria Museum, Kolkata.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and succession
2 CONT
2 CONT Tomb of Humayun Jah at Jafarganj Cemetery.
2 CONT Nawab Nazim Humayun Jah died on 3 October 1838 and was succeeded by his son, Mansur Ali Khan.[1][5]
