1 OCCU Social & Political Activist, Member of the Bombay Municipal Corporation & Bombay Legislative Assembly
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ".. In what ways were the Tyabji's different from a North Indian Muslim family of similar standing? Except few, most of the Tyabji's married among themselves and stayed away from the wealthy, landholding families o
2 CONC f North India or of similar character in Deccan and Western India. [http://www.geni.com/people/Rahat-un-Nafs-Tyabji/6000000011253395566/ Rahat Tyabji] (1849-1905) as the book notes "had strong views about not having her children m
2 CONC arrying into ''Nawabi'' families, but wanted people of good solid bourgeois stock". This had a bearing on how the different branches of the Tyabji's responded to subsequent events in the country. The [http://www.geni.com/people/At
2 CONC iya-Fyzee-Rahamin/6000000011357203705/ Fyzee sisters] had fashioned themselves closely with the ''Urdu''-speaking North Indian elites though it is equally interesting to know that the [http://www.geni.com/people/Atiya-Fyzee-Rahami
2 CONC n/6000000011357203705/ Fyzee sisters] had learnt ''Marathi''. [http://www.geni.com/people/Atiya-Fyzee-Rahamin/6000000011357203705/ Atiya Fyzee] (1877-1967) who later established a school in Bombay would constantly find faults in t
2 CONC he teaching of ''Urdu'' in Bombay’s municipal schools.
2 CONT
2 CONT This led to a cold relationship with [http://www.geni.com/people/Khadija-Tyabji/6000000013352004589/ Khadija Shuffi Tyabji] (1880-1976), who perhaps was the most active woman politician among the Tyabji's, which [http://www.geni.c
2 CONC om/people/Atiya-Fyzee-Rahamin/6000000011357203705/ Atiya] aspired but never managed to emulate. I wish the book had more information about Khadija Tyabji for she was a long running member of the Bombay Municipal Corporation and th
2 CONC e Assembly and was very active on the city’s social and political front. It seems [http://www.geni.com/people/Atiya-Fyzee-Rahamin/6000000011357203705/ Atiya] collaborated closely with Maulana Shaukat Ali though in all fairness I m
2 CONC ust say that [http://www.geni.com/people/Naazli-Begum-Fyzee/6000000011357363083/ Naazli] did collect funds for the Congress as well and was influenced by [http://www.geni.com/people/Mahatma-Gandhi-%E0%A4%AE%E0%A4%B9%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4
2 CONC %A4%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%AE%E0%A4%BE/6000000003922874202/ Gandhi].
2 CONT
2 CONT Khadija was widowed at an early age and her son, [http://www.geni.com/people/Camar-Tyabji/6000000013351820997/ Camar Tyabji] (1904-1962) had settled in Hyderabad where he managed few mills while she remained in Bombay. Khadija was
2 CONC  also associated with the Hajj Committee in the 1930's and did much for the education of women. Just like Khadija, [http://www.geni.com/people/Dilshad-Tyabji/6000000016701843536/ Dilshad Begum] (1879-1924) the daughter of [http://
2 CONC www.geni.com/people/Camaruddin-Tyabji/6000000002006945474/ Camaruddin Tyabji] (1836-1889), remains forgotten. [http://www.geni.com/people/Dilshad-Tyabji/6000000016701843536/ Dilshad] was married into the ''Murshidabad'' royal fami
2 CONC ly. She was active on the restricted and exclusive social scene and her early death in 1925 shocked her friends in Bombay and Bangalore where her daughter, [http://www.geni.com/people/Qamar-Taj-Mirza/6000000016710231915/ Qamar Taj
2 CONC  Mirza] (c. 1900-d?) shifted with her husband. The Sandhurst-educated [http://www.geni.com/people/Syed-Iskander-Ali-Mirza/6000000007143594371/ Iskander Ali Mirza] (1898-1969) who went on to become the 1st President of Pakistan was
2 CONC  her son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 1: Excerpts from the Book, "The Changing World of a Bombay Muslim Community (1870-1945)" by [http://www.geni.com/people/Salima-Tyabji/6000000012815330677/ Salima S. Tyabji].
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 2: http://urdufigures.blogspot.com/2014/01/tyabjis-early-members-of-new-indian.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 3: http://www.nytimes.com/learning/general/specials/india/300525women.html
